<hr />
<h2 id="glossary">Glossary</h2>
<ul>
<li><strong>Backpropagation</strong> — The algorithm that computes gradients of the loss with respect to every parameter by applying the chain rule through the model's computation graph.</li>
<li><strong>Batch gradient descent</strong> — Optimization that computes the gradient over the entire training set before each parameter update; exact but expensive per step.</li>
<li><strong>Batch size</strong> — The number of training examples used in one mini-batch update; affects gradient noise, speed, and memory use.</li>
<li><strong>Convergence</strong> — The state where parameters have settled and the loss no longer decreases meaningfully; the practical end of training.</li>
<li><strong>Divergence</strong> — Training failure in which the loss grows without bound (often to NaN), usually caused by too large a learning rate.</li>
<li><strong>Epoch</strong> — One complete pass over the entire training dataset.</li>
<li><strong>Gradient</strong> — The vector of partial derivatives of the loss with respect to the parameters; points in the direction of steepest increase.</li>
<li><strong>Gradient clipping</strong> — Capping the gradient's magnitude before the update to prevent exploding steps, common in recurrent networks.</li>
<li><strong>Hessian</strong> — The matrix of second derivatives of the loss; describes the curvature of the loss surface in every direction.</li>
<li><strong>Hyperparameter</strong> — A setting chosen before training (learning rate, batch size, momentum) rather than learned by the optimizer.</li>
<li><strong>Iteration (step)</strong> — A single parameter update of the optimizer.</li>
<li><strong>Learning rate</strong> — The scalar η multiplying the (negative) gradient or update direction; controls step size.</li>
<li><strong>Learning rate schedule</strong> — A rule that changes the learning rate during training, e.g., step decay or cosine annealing.</li>
<li><strong>Local minimum</strong> — A point with lower loss than all nearby points, but not necessarily the lowest loss overall.</li>
<li><strong>Loss function</strong> — A differentiable scalar measuring model error on the training data; the quantity the optimizer minimizes.</li>
<li><strong>Mini-batch gradient descent</strong> — Optimization using a small random subset of examples per update; the standard compromise between batch and stochastic methods.</li>
<li><strong>Momentum</strong> — A velocity term accumulating past gradients to accelerate consistent directions and damp oscillations.</li>
<li><strong>Objective function</strong> — The function being optimized; in training, usually the (regularized) training loss.</li>
<li><strong>Overfitting</strong> — Memorizing training data at the expense of generalization; countered by early stopping, regularization, and validation monitoring.</li>
<li><strong>Plateau</strong> — A flat region of the loss surface with near-zero gradients far from a minimum; training appears to stall.</li>
<li><strong>Saddle point</strong> — A point with zero gradient that is a minimum in some directions and a maximum in others; common in high-dimensional landscapes.</li>
<li><strong>Stochastic gradient descent (SGD)</strong> — Optimization using one randomly chosen example (or mini-batch) per update; noisy but cheap steps.</li>
<li><strong>Warmup</strong> — Starting training with a near-zero learning rate that ramps up over early epochs to stabilize initial updates.</li>
<li><strong>Weight decay</strong> — A regularization term penalizing large weights; in AdamW it is decoupled from the adaptive gradient update.</li>
</ul>
<hr />
<h2 id="practice-exercises">Practice Exercises</h2>
<ol>
<li><strong>Evaluate a loss by hand.</strong> For L(w) = (w − 5)², compute L at w = 0, 3, 5, 7. Which is the minimum? Explain in one sentence why brute-force evaluation cannot scale to a neural network with a million parameters.</li>
<li><strong>Read a gradient.</strong> For f(x) = x², compute the gradient at x = −6 and x = 2. For each, state whether gradient descent should increase or decrease x, and why.</li>
<li><strong>One gradient step.</strong> For f(x) = (x − 3)² starting at x₀ = 0 with η = 0.2: compute the gradient, take one step, and verify the loss decreased. (Gradient: 2(x − 3) = −6; x₁ = 0 − 0.2×(−6) = 1.2; loss falls from 9 to 3.24.)</li>
<li><strong>Batch GD on a line.</strong> Data: (1, 2), (2, 4), (3, 6); model y = wx; MSE loss. Derive dL/dw, then starting from w₀ = 0 with η = 0.05, compute two full batch steps and the loss after each. (You should find the gradient is (56/3)(w − 2); w₁ ≈ 0.933, w₂ ≈ 1.246, converging toward w = 2.)</li>
<li><strong>SGD vs. batch on the same problem.</strong> Using the data from Exercise 4, take one SGD step from w₀ = 0 using only the example (2, 4) with η = 0.05. Compare your w₁ with the batch w₁ from Exercise 4 and explain the difference in one or two sentences.</li>
<li><strong>Learning rate regimes.</strong> For f(x) = x² starting at x₀ = 8, compute two steps with (a) η = 0.05, (b) η = 1.0, (c) η = 1.2. Classify each as converging smoothly, oscillating, or diverging, and state the loss after two steps in each case.</li>
<li><strong>Momentum by hand.</strong> For f(x) = x², x₀ = 6, v₀ = 0, η = 0.1, β = 0.9: compute two momentum updates (v ← βv + g; x ← x − ηv). Compare with two plain GD steps from the same start and explain which is faster and why.</li>
<li><strong>Adam's bias correction.</strong> With gradients g₁ = 4, g₂ = 4 and defaults (β₁ = 0.9, β₂ = 0.999, α = 0.001): compute m, v, m̂, v̂ after step 1, and the resulting parameter change. Then compute what the change would be <em>without</em> bias correction, and explain why the correction matters most early in training.</li>
<li><strong>Design a schedule.</strong> You will train for 60 epochs with a maximum learning rate of 0.02. Write out (a) a step-decay schedule that drops by 10× at epochs 20 and 40, and (b) the cosine-annealing learning rate at epochs 0, 15, 30, 45, 60 (η_min = 0). Which would you choose for best final accuracy, and why?</li>
<li><strong>Write the reproducibility paragraph.</strong> Pick a model you have trained (or imagine one: a small CNN on 5,000 images). Write a 6–8 sentence optimization paragraph covering all 10 checklist items from Chapter 12. Then re-read it as a skeptical reviewer and list two details you would still ask for.</li>
</ol>
